Hi Niels,

Ideally I think dplyr should handle that for you, but I don't think
tools aren't there in DBI. I filed an issue:
https://github.com/rstats-db/DBI/issues/64

Hadley

On Fri, Nov 13, 2015 at 9:54 AM, Niels Schenk <schenk...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Hi Hadly,
>
> Actually I'm pretty sure that the issue arises because my database
> connection is timing out. I was just wondering whether anyone has dealt with
> this issue when this happens. What I've done now is set the timeout of my
> database to be larger than the Shiny app but I'm sometimes still running
> into issues when my Shiny app doesn't use the database connections.
>
> Thanks,
>
> Niels
>
> On Friday, November 13, 2015 at 4:41:46 PM UTC+1, Hadley Wickham wrote:
>>
>> Are you sure the issue is dplyr disconnecting and not a timeout on the
>> database server?
>> Hadley
>>
>> On Fri, Nov 13, 2015 at 9:16 AM, Niels Schenk <schenk...@gmail.com> wrote:
>> > Hi,
>> >
>> > I'm running into an issue that I can't seem to resolve. I'm using dplyr
>> > in a
>> > Shiny app. When I leave the app open for a moderately long time but I'm
>> > not
>> > actually interacting with the app, dplyr disconnects the connection to
>> > my
>> > Postgres database. This probably happens when dplyr is garbage
>> > collecting.
>> > I've tried to modify src_postgres() in such a way that disco =
>> > db_disconnector(con, "postgres") is taken out of the call to src_sql so
>> > that
>> > I can handle the disconnections myself. Unfortunately this doesn't help.
>> >
>> > The other thing I've tried was that instead of feeding my tbl()
>> > definitions
>> > a call to an object created by src_postgres(), I fed them with a custom
>> > function that checks whether the connection to the database is still
>> > open.
>> > Although the function itself works fine, the objects created by tbl()
>> > don't
>> > reuse the function but inherit the database connection settings when the
>> > function is initially run. This means that when for example performing a
>> > filter on a tbl() object, the connection to the database is not checked
>> > but
>> > I'm always using the connection from src_postgres that was initially run
>> > when I created the tbl object. This means that when dplyr disconnection
>> > my
>> > initial connection to postgres, refreshing the connection doesn't make
>> > sense.
>> >
>> > I would really appreciate some guidance on how to elegantly solve this.
>> > The
>> > only option I see for now is to create functions of all my tbl
>> > definitions
>> > to check for open connection but this seems like hitting a needle with a
>> > hammer.
>> >
>> > An example of what I'm using now based on
>> > https://groups.google.com/d/topic/shiny-discuss/0VjQc2a6z3M/discussion:
>> >
>> >
>> > dbConnection() <- function() {
>> >
>> >   if (!exists('connection')) {
>> >     connection <<- src_postgres(dbname = "postgres",
>> >                               host = postgres,
>> >                               port = 5432, user = "postgres", password =
>> > "password")
>> >   } else if (class(try(RPostgreSQL::dbGetQuery(connection$con, "SELECT 1
>> > from table"))) == "try-error") {
>> >     RPostgreSQL::dbDisconnect(connection$con)
>> >     connection <<- src_postgres(dbname = "postgres",
>> >                               host = postgres,
>> >                               port = 5432, user = "postgres", password =
>> > "password")
>> >   }
>> >
>> >   return(connection)
>> > }
>> >
>> > my_tbl <- tbl(dbConnection(), "my_table")
>> >
>> >
>> >
>> > Many thanks for any help,
>> >
>> > Niels
>> >
